# PART A - SUPERVISED LEARNING

Supervised learning maps inputs to known outputs using labeled data.

- Input X, label y
- Goal: learn function f: X -> y

## Linear Regression

### Beginner Intuition
- Predict a continuous value using a weighted sum of features.
- Example: predict house price from size and rooms.

### Core Theory
- Model: y = Xw + b
- Loss: Mean Squared Error (MSE) = (1/n) sum (y - y_hat)^2
- Closed-form solution: w = (X^T X)^-1 X^T y (when invertible)

### AI Relevance
- Baseline for regression; helps understand bias/variance.

### Industry Example
- Pricing engines for real estate or used cars.

### Common Interview Questions & Answers
1. When should you use linear regression? — When the relationship is approx. linear and interpretability is important.
2. Why prefer solving linear systems over inverse? — Numerical stability and efficiency (use QR or solver routines).

---

## Logistic Regression

### Beginner Intuition
- Predict probability of a binary outcome using a linear score passed through sigmoid.
- Example: predict whether a customer will churn (yes/no).

### Core Theory
- Model: p(y=1|x) = sigmoid(w^T x + b)
- Loss: Binary cross-entropy / log-loss
- Decision boundary is linear in input space.

### AI Relevance
- Interpretable classifier, baseline for many tasks.

### Industry Example
- Fraud detection (binary: fraud / not fraud) as a simple first model.

### Common Interview Questions & Answers
1. What does the sigmoid output mean? — Probability of positive class.
2. How to handle multiclass? — Use softmax (multinomial logistic) or one-vs-rest.

---

## Decision Trees

### Beginner Intuition
- Split data by feature thresholds to create human-readable rules.
- Think of a flowchart of yes/no questions.

### Core Theory
- Build tree by greedy splitting (maximizing information gain or reducing Gini impurity).
- Prone to overfitting; control by max depth, min samples leaf.

### Diagram
```mermaid
flowchart TD
  A[Start] --> B{Feature <= t}
  B -- yes --> C[Leaf: predict 1]
  B -- no --> D[Leaf: predict 0]
```

### AI Relevance
- Explainable models, fast inference, handles mixed types natively.

### Industry Example
- Credit decision rules and feature importance explanation.

### Common Interview Questions & Answers
1. Why do trees overfit? — They can create very specific rules that fit noise; regularize with pruning.
2. How to interpret feature importance? — Measures like mean decrease in impurity approximate feature relevance.

---

## Random Forest

### Beginner Intuition
- Ensemble of many decision trees trained on bootstrapped samples and random feature subsets; averages predictions.

### Core Theory
- Bagging + feature randomness reduces variance and overfitting compared to single tree.

### Differentiation Table: Tree vs Random Forest

| Property | Decision Tree | Random Forest |
|---|---:|---:|
| Variance | High | Lower |
| Interpretability | High | Lower |
| Training Time | Fast | Slower |

### AI Relevance
- Strong off-the-shelf performer for tabular data.

### Industry Example
- Churn prediction ensembles used in production with feature importances for stakeholders.

### Common Interview Questions & Answers
1. Why does random forest reduce variance? — Averaging many uncorrelated trees cancels out individual errors.
2. When to use RF vs GBM? — RF for robust baseline, GBM for fine-tuned predictive performance.

---

## XGBoost / Gradient Boosting Machines (GBM)

### Beginner Intuition
- Build trees sequentially where each new tree corrects previous residuals (boosting).

### Core Theory
- Optimize additive model via gradient descent on loss in function space.
- Regularization, tree constraints, and learning rate control overfitting.

### Diagram
```mermaid
flowchart LR
  Input --> T1[Tree1]
  Input --> T2[Tree2]
  T1 --> Sum[Sum predictions]
  T2 --> Sum
  Sum --> Output
```

### Differentiation Table: RF vs GBM vs Linear

| Property | Random Forest | Gradient Boosting (XGB) | Linear Regression |
|---|---:|---:|---:|
| Handles nonlinearity | Yes | Yes | No |
| Tuning complexity | Low | High | Low |
| Best for tabular | Yes | Often best | Baseline |

### AI Relevance
- State-of-the-art on many tabular benchmarks; used in competitions and production.

### Industry Example
- Credit scoring, recommendation ranking features, high-stakes leaderboards.

### Common Interview Questions & Answers
1. What is learning rate in boosting? — Step size for each tree's contribution; small rates require more trees but can generalize better.
2. How to avoid overfitting in GBM? — Lower learning rate, limit tree depth, use early stopping and regularization.

---

## Support Vector Machines (SVM)

### Beginner Intuition
- Find a hyperplane that maximizes margin between classes; kernel trick allows nonlinear boundaries.

### Core Theory
- Solve convex optimization (hinge loss + regularization).
- Kernels: linear, polynomial, RBF map inputs to higher-dimensional spaces.

### AI Relevance
- Good for smaller datasets and high-dimensional spaces (text).

### Industry Example
- Text classification with TF-IDF features.

### Common Interview Questions & Answers
1. Why use kernels? — To model nonlinear separations without explicit feature engineering.
2. What's the role of C parameter? — Balances margin maximization and training error (regularization strength).

---

## k-Nearest Neighbors (k-NN)

### Beginner Intuition
- Predict label by majority vote (classification) or average (regression) of k closest training examples.

### Core Theory
- Distance-based, lazy learner (no training beyond storing data), sensitive to feature scales.

### AI Relevance
- Simple baseline; good for small datasets and intuitive recommendations.

### Industry Example
- Collaborative filtering for small-scale recommendation prototypes.

### Common Interview Questions & Answers
1. How to choose k? — Cross-validation; small k -> noisy, large k -> smoother/potentially biased.
2. Why scale features for k-NN? — Distance dominated by large-scale features otherwise.

