# PART B - UNSUPERVISED LEARNING

No labels — discover structure in data.

## K-Means Clustering

### Beginner Intuition
- Partition data into k clusters by assigning points to nearest centroid and updating centroids.

### Core Theory
- Minimize within-cluster sum of squares via iterative Lloyd's algorithm.

### Diagram
```mermaid
flowchart LR
  Data --> Assign[Assign to nearest centroid]
  Assign --> Update[Update centroids]
  Update --> Repeat[Repeat until converge]
```

### AI Relevance
- Segment users, compress data, initialize other algorithms.

### Industry Example
- Customer segmentation for targeted marketing.

### Common Interview Questions & Answers
1. How to choose k? — Elbow method, silhouette score, business constraints.
2. Limitations? — Assumes spherical clusters, sensitive to initialization.

---

## PCA (Principal Component Analysis)

### Beginner Intuition
- Rotate and project data to directions of maximum variance and keep top components.

### Core Theory
- Eigen decomposition of covariance or SVD; choose top-k components by explained variance.

### Diagram
```mermaid
graph LR
  Data[Raw data] --> SVD[SVD / Eigendecomp]
  SVD --> Components[Top components]
  Components --> Project[Project data]
```

### AI Relevance
- Dimensionality reduction, noise reduction, visualization.

### Common Interview Questions & Answers
1. How many components to keep? — Keep enough to explain desired variance (e.g., 90-95%) or use scree plot.
2. PCA vs t-SNE for visualization? — PCA is linear and preserves variance; t-SNE preserves local neighborhood structure for visualization.

---

## Other Unsupervised Methods (Brief)
- Hierarchical clustering, DBSCAN, Autoencoders for nonlinear compression.

