# PART C - MODEL EVALUATION

Choose metrics and validation strategies aligned with business goals.

## Confusion Matrix & Derived Metrics

- Confusion matrix: TP, FP, FN, TN

Precision = TP / (TP + FP)

Recall = TP / (TP + FN)

F1 = 2 * (precision * recall) / (precision + recall)

### Diagram (confusion)
```mermaid
flowchart LR
  PredPos[Predicted Positive] -->|TP| TruePos[True Positive]
  PredPos -->|FP| FalsePos[False Positive]
  PredNeg[Predicted Negative] -->|FN| FalseNeg[False Negative]
  PredNeg -->|TN| TrueNeg[True Negative]
```

### Common Interview Questions & Answers
1. When prefer precision over recall? — Precision when false positives are costly (spam flagging business loss).
2. When prefer recall? — Recall when missing positives is costly (disease screening).

---

## ROC-AUC and PR Curves

- ROC-AUC: tradeoff across thresholds (TPR vs FPR). Good for balanced classes.
- PR curve: precision vs recall, better for imbalanced positives.

### Common Interview Questions & Answers
1. Why PR curve for imbalanced data? — PR emphasizes positive class performance and shows precision at relevant recall values.
2. Is higher AUC always better? — Higher AUC indicates better ranking, but practical thresholds and calibration matter.

---

## Cross-Validation and Data Leakage

- K-fold CV, stratified CV, time-series CV (rolling windows)
- Data leakage: any use of test info in training (targets, aggregated stats) invalidates evaluation.

### Common Interview Questions & Answers
1. How to prevent leakage? — Split data early, compute transforms on train only, avoid target-derived features across splits.
2. Why use stratified CV? — Preserve class balance in folds for classification.

